      <h1>What Happens After Receiving a Notice to Appear in Immigration Court?</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A Notice to Appear, Form I-862, is the document that starts a removal case in immigration court. It tells you who is prosecuting you, on what grounds you are alleged to be removable, and when and where to appear. Appear at that hearing. Failing to appear after proper written notice lets the immigration judge order you removed in absentia, and under 8 U.S.C. §1229a(b)(7) a missing hearing raises the bar to a decade on cancellation of removal, voluntary departure, adjustment of status, and other listed forms of discretionary relief.</p>
<p>Receiving the NTA is not the same as being ordered removed. It is the opening move of a case you now have to play.</p>
<h2>What the Notice to Appear Actually Says</h2>
<p>Form I-862 comes out of the Department of Homeland Security, usually through USCIS or ICE. EOIR's plain-language guide to the NTA is at <a href="https://www.justice.gov/eoir/notice-appear">justice.gov/eoir/notice-appear</a>, and USCIS describes the form on its <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/save/current-user-agencies/commonly-used-immigration-documents">commonly used immigration documents</a> page.</p>
<p>Read it line by line, because every block sets something in motion:</p>
<ul>
<li>The nature of the proceeding, usually proceedings to remove you based on one or more charges.</li>
<li>The factual allegations against you, stated in the document as the government's case.</li>
<li>The grounds of removability, each tied to a statutory section of Title 8.</li>
<li>The date, time, and place of your hearing, if one has been set.</li>
<li>A statement of your right to be represented by an attorney or authorized representative at no cost to you.</li>
<li>A warning about the consequences of not appearing.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the allegations are wrong, the place to fight that is at the hearing, in the form of a pleading that admits or denies each allegation. Guessing at the paperwork accomplishes nothing.</p>
<h2>Does Your NTA Include a Court Date?</h2>
<p>Sometimes. Plenty of respondents get an NTA with no hearing information at all, and that is not a sign that nothing is scheduled. The court often sends the hearing notice separately, and EOIR describes it as a Notice of Hearing.</p>
<p>Two timing rules matter here. EOIR's own practice rules set a floor on scheduling: the first hearing date will not be set earlier than 10 days after the NTA was served. So a short turnaround between the date on the paperwork and the date on your hearing notice is not automatically a mistake, though you should still confirm it.</p>
<p>The second rule is the Supreme Court's decision in <em>Pereira v. Sessions</em>, 585 U.S. 198 (2018). An NTA that does not state a specific time and place does not trigger the stop-time provision that would otherwise cut off the 10-year clock you might need for cancellation of removal. If your paperwork never named a date and a place, that fact is worth knowing on day one, not in your tenth month.</p>
<h2>The First Hearing Is a Master Calendar Hearing</h2>
<p>The initial hearing is usually a master calendar hearing. It is administrative. Pleadings get entered, the case gets scheduled, the judge confirms what relief you intend to raise, and the judge sets filing deadlines. You are not expected to present your whole case that morning.</p>
<img src="/assets/blog/immigration-courtroom-hearing.webp" alt="An empty immigration courtroom seen from the public gallery, with the judge bench and flags at the front" class="blog-post-image" loading="lazy" />
<p>A typical master calendar hearing goes roughly like this, and this is an illustrative composite rather than any specific client matter: the judge calls the case, you confirm your name and address, the government states the allegations, your attorney responds through a pleading, the judge notes whether the charge is being contested, and your attorney says which applications you intend to file and by when. The judge then sets a second date. That exchange often takes a few minutes, which is exactly why people walk out of the building without realising they just agreed to a filing schedule.</p>
<p>Later, the case moves to an individual calendar hearing, the merits hearing, where evidence and testimony are actually taken.</p>
<h2>Your Right to a Lawyer, and the Notario Trap</h2>
<p>You have the right to be represented at no expense to the government. What you do not have is a public defender. Immigration court does not appoint counsel the way a criminal court might, and the judge cannot order DHS to pay for your attorney. If you cannot afford one, the realistic options are a private attorney, a nonprofit legal services organisation, or a law school clinic that takes removal cases.</p>
<p>Be careful who you hire. A notario is not an immigration lawyer. Notarios in California are authorised to prepare documents, not to practise law, give legal advice, or represent anyone in court. The same limitation applies to anyone who is not admitted to practise before the immigration courts and the Board of Immigration Appeals, or recognised and accredited by the Department of Justice. Ask for bar admission or accreditation numbers, and verify them, before you hand over your documents and your fee.</p>
<h2>What Happens If You Do Not Appear</h2>
<p>This is the part of the process that ends most cases by accident, so read it plainly. Under 8 U.S.C. §1229a(b)(5)(A), when a respondent fails to appear after proper written notice, the immigration judge shall order the respondent removed in absentia if the Department of Homeland Security proves two things by clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence: that notice was proper, and that the respondent is removable.</p>
<p><em>In absentia</em> is Latin for "in absence." It is a real removal order, entered without you, based on the paper record.</p>
<p>The notice requirement is thinner than most people assume. Under §1229a(b)(5)(B), if you failed to provide your address as required by §1229a(a)(1)(F), then written notice is not required at all, and an in absentia order can still issue. Keeping your address current with the court is not housekeeping. It is the thing that makes notice provable.</p>
<p>Then the second wave hits. Under §1229a(b)(7), a respondent who misses a hearing after being told orally about the consequences of failing to appear is barred for 10 years from cancellation of removal, voluntary departure, adjustment of status, a change of nonimmigrant status, and a record of lawful admission — sections 1229b, 1229c, 1255, 1258, and 1259 of Title 8. The statute defines "exceptional circumstances" at §1229a(e)(1), and that definition is what a motion to reopen has to satisfy.</p>
<h2>What If I Already Missed My Hearing?</h2>
<p>Missed hearings are not all equal, and the law treats them differently depending on why the order issued. The reopen and reconsider deadlines come from 8 U.S.C. §1229a(c)(7)(C)(i) with <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-8/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-1003/section-1003.2">8 CFR 1003.2(c)(2)</a> for reopening, and §1229a(c)(6)(B) with 8 CFR 1003.2(b)(2) for reconsideration.</p>
