<p>The no-time-limit reopen for defective notice under §1229a(b)(5)(C)(ii) is the most forgiving line in the table, and it connects directly to <em>Pereira</em>. If the NTA that started this never specified a time and place, the argument that you were never properly noticed is a live one.</p>
<p>On the BIA appeal line, note that this deadline moved twice in 2026. The February 6, 2026 interim final rule on appellate procedures (91 FR 5267) cut it to 10 days. On March 8, 2026 the federal district court in <em>Amica Center for Immigrant Rights v. EOIR</em> set that provision aside for failure to follow notice-and-comment rulemaking, and EOIR's Policy Memorandum 26-02 then confirmed the deadline stands at 30 days. As of October 2026 that is still the operative rule, and EOIR has said it will reissue the regulation through proper rulemaking — so confirm the number in effect on your decision date before you rely on it. Do not calculate a filing date off a summary you read online. Pull the current text and check the docket.</p>
<p>Two practical points about all of these filings. First, a motion to reopen and rescind an in absentia order — filed within the 180-day window for exceptional circumstances, or at any time for lack of proper notice — automatically stays removal while the judge decides it. A motion to reopen for any other reason carries no such stay, so ask about one before you rely on it. Second, a BIA appeal does not stop execution either, and there is no automatic stay. That last point matters more than it sounds. A removal order executed and a person gone does not undo itself later. If your date is close, raise the stay question in the first conversation rather than after the filing.</p>
<h2>The Forms of Relief You Might Be Planning to Raise</h2>
<p>Your attorney will raise your applications at the master calendar hearing and prove them at the merits hearing. Knowing the names now helps you tell your story in a straight line.</p>
<ul>
<li>Cancellation of removal, 8 U.S.C. §1229b(b). For respondents who are not lawful permanent residents, this requires 10 years of continuous physical presence in the United States, good moral character during that period, and that removal would result in "exceptional and extremely unusual hardship" to you or to a qualifying family member. That hardship standard is among the hardest in immigration law, and <em>Pereira</em> matters here because the 10-year clock stops when a properly served NTA states a specific time and place.</li>
<li>Voluntary departure, 8 U.S.C. §1229c. The judge grants permission to leave instead of being removed, on conditions, with a defined departure date and a reentry bar. Departure terms are often worth negotiating at the right moment rather than refusing outright.</li>
<li>Asylum. One-year deadline to apply from the date of entry. Miss it and the claim is gone, with very narrow exceptions for things like a disabled child, a family member already granted asylum, or changed country conditions.</li>
<li>Withholding of removal. No one-year deadline and no registration bar, but a higher evidentiary burden and no protection outside the United States. If the country conditions are the problem rather than the border crossing, this is often the realistic path.</li>
<li>Adjustment of status. For some respondents already inside the country, a lawful admission is the cleanest route, if the government does not object.</li>
</ul>
<p>Which of these you qualify for turns on your immigration history, family situation, any criminal record, and your prior filings. That is a case-specific question, and it is also the reason the first meeting should be a long one.</p>
<h2>Bond Hearings and Release on Supervision Are Different Things</h2>
<p>These two come up together constantly and they are not the same thing. Under 8 U.S.C. §1226(a), a bond hearing happens in immigration court to set the amount of bond for a respondent in DHS custody, or to decide whether release should come with conditions instead. It is a judicial determination about money and custody conditions.</p>
<p>Under 8 U.S.C. §1231(a)(6), the decision is administrative. Once a removal order is final and the 90-day removal period has passed without DHS being able to carry it out, DHS decides on its own whether to keep detaining you or release you — and release under that provision comes with terms of supervision: where you live, where you report, and how often. No judge sets a dollar figure, because bond is not the mechanism. If you are handed paperwork with reporting obligations instead of a bond amount, that is supervision under §1231(a)(6), not a bond.</p>
<h2>Where the Hearing Happens</h2>
