<p>Hearings are scheduled by the court rather than by you, and venue, courtroom assignments, and building locations change. Confirm your hearing venue directly with the court before you travel, using the case status number printed on your NTA or hearing notice.</p>
<h2>How to Check Your Case Status</h2>
<p>Do not rely on a letter that never arrives. You have three ways to check where your case stands.</p>
<ul>
<li>EOIR's automated case status line: 1-800-898-7180, or 304-625-2050, with TDD at 800-828-1120. It gives the next hearing date, the type of hearing, and the current waiting room. Have your nine-digit alien number ready.</li>
<li>The EOIR Respondent Access portal, which is also where you report an address change to the court.</li>
<li>USCIS Case Status Online at <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/case-status">uscis.gov/case-status</a>, for any USCIS filings in your history — petitions and applications. It does not show immigration court hearing dates; that is EOIR's system.</li>
</ul>
<p>EOIR's public information page on the notice to appear is at <a href="https://www.justice.gov/eoir/notice-appear">justice.gov/eoir/notice-appear</a>.</p>
<p>Two rules for the portal. A change of address filed through Respondent Access updates EOIR but does not by itself serve as notice to DHS, so tell your attorney too. And keep screenshots of everything. If the date on the portal and the date on your paper differ, raise it immediately.</p>
<h2>Documents to Gather Now</h2>
<p>Start collecting these before your attorney asks, because some of them take weeks to obtain and every day of delay is a day on a filing clock.</p>
<ul>
<li>The NTA and every notice the court has sent you, including the envelopes.</li>
<li>Your passport and any other identity documents, current and expired.</li>
<li>Every prior immigration application and every decision letter, including denials and requests for evidence.</li>
<li>Marriage, birth, and partnership certificates for every family member who figures into your case.</li>
<li>Passport entry stamps or I-94 records showing exactly when you entered, because the one-year asylum deadline and the 10-year presence clock both run from entry dates.</li>
<li>Employment and tax records, showing continuous presence.</li>
<li>Medical records, if you are claiming a serious medical condition.</li>
<li>Police records and court dispositions for any arrest, however minor, plus proof of any disposition that closes it.</li>
<li>Country conditions reports, news coverage, and expert letters, if you are claiming persecution or withholding of removal.</li>
<li>Proof of any bond, motion, or filing you have already made in this case.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Does a Notice to Appear include a court date?</h3>
<p>Sometimes. Plenty of respondents receive an NTA with no hearing information at all, and a blank date field does not mean nothing is scheduled — the court often sends the hearing notice separately. Check your case with EOIR's automated line at 1-800-898-7180 or the Respondent Access portal, using your nine-digit A-number.</p>
<h3>What happens at the first immigration court hearing?</h3>
<p>It is a master calendar hearing, and it is short. The judge confirms the charges, takes your pleadings, notes which forms of relief you intend to raise, and sets filing deadlines and your next date. You are not expected to present your whole case that morning.</p>
<h3>Do I get a lawyer in immigration court?</h3>
<p>You have the right to be represented, but the court does not appoint counsel and the government does not pay for one. The realistic options are a private attorney, a nonprofit legal services organization, or a law school clinic that takes removal cases.</p>
<h3>What happens if I miss my hearing?</h3>
<p>If notice was proper, the judge can order you removed in absentia — a real removal order entered without you — and that order bars cancellation of removal, voluntary departure, adjustment of status, and related relief for 10 years. A motion to rescind must be filed within 180 days for exceptional circumstances, or may be filed at any time for lack of proper notice.</p>
<h3>How long do I have to appeal an immigration judge's decision?</h3>
<p>30 days, as of October 2026. A February 2026 interim final rule cut the deadline to 10 days, but a federal court set that provision aside on March 8, 2026, and EOIR's Policy Memorandum 26-02 confirms the deadline stands at 30 days. Confirm the deadline in effect on your decision date before filing.</p>
<h3>How do I check my case status?</h3>
<p>Three ways: call EOIR's automated case information line at 1-800-898-7180 (TDD 800-828-1120) with your nine-digit A-number ready, check the EOIR Respondent Access portal online, or use USCIS Case Status Online for any USCIS filings in your history.</p>
<h2>Talk to an Immigration Attorney in San Francisco</h2>
<p>Here is the honest version of what a lawyer does in the first weeks. We read the NTA and tell you which allegations matter and which you should concede. We calculate your asylum one-year date and your continuous presence date before either one passes. We check whether the NTA you were served actually stated a time and place, because that single detail decides whether <em>Pereira</em> protects your 10-year clock. We check your criminal and immigration history for the kind of problems that quietly sink otherwise strong applications. And we tell you plainly when a case is weak.</p>
<p>If your NTA came with no date, or the date is less than 10 days out, or you have already missed something, that is the conversation to have today, not next month. Denials at the consular stage and removal proceedings are different problems with different clocks, and <a href="/blog/what-happens-if-your-visa-application-is-denied/">what happens after a visa denial</a> will not tell you what happens here. If your concern is the interview stage instead, our piece on <a href="/blog/what-happens-during-a-uscis-interview/">what happens during a USCIS interview</a> covers that process.</p>
